#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166437 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "a19f597d-f584-57b3-9e67-cb4cea07c876" :question/math-academy-id "e-21120" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n1. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 4x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{1}(t) + 6x_{2}(t)$\n2. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 7x_{2}(t)$\n3. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 5x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 2x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, $x'_1(t) = 4x_1(t) + 2x_2(t)$ depends on $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$.\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, $x'_1(t) = -3x_1(t)$ depends on only $x_1(t)$ and $x'_2(t) = 7x_2(t)$ depends on only $x_2(t)$.\n- System III is *not* decoupled because, for example, the first equation depends on $x_2(t)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166438 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce35e5a9-2444-5e5f-9463-5a4d54496d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Decoupled Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "83ebd59d-883f-510a-9bc9-035b3088a43c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21123" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations given by\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-6x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3x_{2}(t)$." :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nthe formula for the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, we write our differential equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of the differential system is diagonal, the system is decoupled.\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166439 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cec0e48f-9fbb-5ada-b7fd-0d389d1b7438" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Decoupled Linear System of First-Order ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "f5136df2-fcf6-5f64-aeda-f14a91231788" :question/math-academy-id "e-21124" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\ln (3)\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{1t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2\\ln (3)t} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}(e^{\\ln (9)})^{t} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}9^{t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial value $\\mathbf{x}(0)$. Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}9^{0} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + (-1)\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}9^{t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}9^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86d627ea-fc98-5e90-9321-9ba02fcb5a2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163961} {:answer/id #uuid "a7aa757a-81d9-560a-9bb7-cc7ae32812a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163962} {:answer/id #uuid "ddcd8edd-af1c-5002-b03d-403cae009a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163963} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c25bcf-3448-5050-abb2-38cc402775f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163964} {:answer/id #uuid "56b92791-8adc-5a75-b183-fc76558135cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3c25bcf-3448-5050-abb2-38cc402775f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163964} :answer-field/id #uuid "421ef6b7-af3f-503e-a607-9df5e3b3125d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163960}] :question/id #uuid "ed1ca0ce-9964-570a-9175-5b218c92ebb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-321897" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n1. $x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n2. $x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n3. $x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id